WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
we catch up with you here in south  africa your last stop on your first  africa trip you started in senegal you  went on to niger your predecessor angela  merkel put a focus on africa what does  it feel like for you now to touch down  here for the first time as german  chancellor  africa is the continent  next to europe and it is  of essence that we work intensely to  have very good relations with all the  states in africa and also it is  important that we develop  our relations with all the democratic  states on the globe democracy the rule  of law questions that are important for  us are not just something that is  linked with the so-called western  countries it's also  very important if we look at all the  countries in the world so south africa  is a democracy senegal new year and this  is why i visited them and why i think it  is important that we  continue to work together  saying that the world has a good future  if we cooperate  if we understand that there will be a  lot of very  relevant states in the future in the 50s  of this century and that we should now  make it feasible that there is a good  cooperation that we can create a  multilateral world  now the shadow of the ukraine conflict  looms large here  this is europe being very much busy with  itself there's a war in europe  and at the same time here many countries  particularly those that you visited  are set to feel the brunt of the effects  there's a looming hunger crisis  fuel shortages fertilizer shortages  what responsibility does germany feel  to save countries that have nothing to  do with this conflict from the effects  first  russian russia  invaded ukraine  started a brutal war against the  neighbor country and so it is important  that we stick together to give the  ukraine the chance to defend itself its  integrity its sovereignty  and this is also important for peace on  the globe we should agree that there  should never again  be a successful attempt to  change borders  with force and this is what  russia is trying to do and this is why  we cannot accept this and this is why so  many countries are supporting ukraine  with financial means with  weapons also and  we developed the sanctions regime  against russia  to convince the russian leadership that  they should go away from the war that  they should start  negotiations with the ukraine and that  they  withdraw their troops from ukraine  territory where they invaded  the country  and on the other hand it is obviously  that  it is obvious that a lot of countries  are suffering from this war and from the  russian aggression and this is why we  are  very  decided that we will help the countries  that are suffering this is why we  started to cooperate in fighting against  all the consequences of  having not enough food of  supporting the countries with their own  economic difficulties they have and this  is why we also speak about how we can  work together in this very difficult  crisis but the best thing to do is  ending the war and if russia stopped its  aggression and  would  be ready for a peace this would be the  best for the whole globe what concrete  assistance can countries like for  instance sudan somalia niger expect  we are working on  supporting the world food programme and  we are  starting to gather an initiative against  the shortages that are coming  up now and we will work together with  the countries on the uni global level  together with the united nations  and this is what we are  trying to convince all  other countries that have the means to  do so to do together with us your trade  minister robert harbeck also spoke about  a u.s eu initiative  to  lower fuel prices worldwide can you  flesh that out of it  we will have  a  very difficult situation if we look at  fuel prices  obviously this will be not feasible to  subsidize them  down  and  even not on the global level but it is  necessary that we  start to increase the supply with gas  with fuel with all the things to make it  feasible for the countries to pay for  the build  so how will you get  fuel shortages  off the table reduce prices  we are now discussing with all these  countries that are  exploring oil and gas and  trying to convince them to increase  their capacities so this would help the  world market  now it's three months to the day  today that russia invaded ukraine you're  one of the very few world leaders who  actually still have a direct line to  vladimir putin  vladimir zelinski ukraine's president at  the world economic forum spoke of  putin's intention to cause maximum  damage the maximum loss of lives  many people are wondering whether putin  has lost touch with reality what is your  impression when you speak to him  this is a  brutal aggression  a very brutal war  and  it is really an imperialistic approach  he tries to conquer parts of the  territory of his neighbor country  and this is what we will not be will be  never we will never accept and this is  why it is necessary that we support the  ukraine as i already  mentioned and that we on the other hand  talk directly to putin saying him that  he should stop the war and that  the activity he is starting there the  war  will never have a good outcome for his  own country we should not  forget that the  sanctions for instance  are  having a very heavy impact on the  russian economy  today but also in the future they will  go back  in their opportunities of economic  development  for decades so they are will suffer and  they are suffering and for getting out  of this he has to stop the war  now the united nations says for the  first time there's some 100 million  people fleeing conflict on the move  around the globe  germany is negotiating also with many  countries to take illegal so-called  illegal migration migrants back  what will germany do what will you do as  g7 presidency  to open avenues for legal migration  we have to be aware that there are a lot  of  people  that  are in a very desperate situation that  are fleeing from the places they lived  because of war because of a lot of other  reasons and so we have a global  responsibility to support the refugees  where they are and this is what we  never should forget that there is a  responsibility not just starting if for  instance refugees are coming to the  western countries and trying to live  there we also have to do a lot at the  places where they live in  most cases they're just  fled to the neighbor country and this  neighbor country needs assistance for  being able to cope with the situation  on the other hand  it is obvious that there will be the  need for legal ways of migration for  instance to europe and that we have to  find a way to  cooperate with the countries where the  people are looking for the chances to to  start a better life in  european countries in other countries of  the world and  doing this is then also an answer to  fight against illegal migration because  this also needs cooperation between the  countries  making it feasible that people go back  to the country of origin if they cannot  stay in the country they wanted to go  i just want to talk about the sahara  region because it's a great source of  instability that leads to migration as  well how concerned are you that you  might be supporting the wrong side by  extending one of the mandates in mali  after the coup  we are supporting the united nations  with their activity in  sahel and especially in mali with this  so-called menusma mandate  and this is something that is good for  stabilization in the region  but we also state very clear that we  will never accept  a situation  where  there will be a fight with  the support of russian soldiers in mali  against the people so it is necessary  that they are going back to the past of  a democratic  perspective  you speak of a special responsibility  that you feel for contributing to the  future being a multi-polar world  how does germany's europe's colonial  past shape the way  you want to integrate particularly  african countries  germany but  all countries with the  colonial past  have to be very honest and admitting  that this past is  part of their history and that they have  responsibility for better relations with  the countries  for instance  in africa  and  doing this is also the basis for a good  relationship in the future and my view  is that the world will have a lot of  very influential countries  in the next decades that  are not just  russia china united states and europe  for instance it will be also a lot of  other countries and this is one of the  reasons why i for instance invited  indonesia and india  senegal and south africa or argentine  to  participate in our g7 meeting in germany  this year and this summer  because it's a starting point for  understanding the world it's something  that we have responsibility together for  and this is the basis for  a multilateral world where we not just  see a lot of different influential  countries but countries that cooperate  for a better future  what role does africa play in this are  you more up against china or russia here  without vision  africa  is  a continent with a lot of  countries that and states that  will have a  future that  is better than today that will have a  lot of people living there and so it is  necessary that we  do all which is  feasible to  make it happen that this will be a good  future and cooperation  economic cooperation  support in the development is the basis  for this what most surprised you on this  trip  it was very  good to discuss with the leaders and  understanding that they have a very  similar view to the world and that they  are also  sharing the willingness  of cooperation and this is  one of the outcomes of this  trip to three countries and just finally  just a few kilometers from where we are  sitting right now nelson mandela was  elected the first democratically elected  president of south africa  if you could meet him today  what would you tell him or ask him  he was really a very great leader  and working intensely for freedom and  democracy and  i think  there is not a question i should have  asked him  but the question all asked him really is  how did you make it to state that  situation  in the jail for that long time and  keeping to be a very democratic leader  it is really a great man  olaf schwarz chancellor of germany on  his first trip to africa thank you very  much thank you  you

